---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Research"
  description="Research projects of Sai Vijay Mocherla — gas–surface scattering, machine-learning interatomic potentials, ultrafast electron dynamics, and quantum transport."
>
  <article class="container research">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="kicker mono">Research</p>
      <h1>Projects &amp; publications</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        My work sits at the intersection of chemical physics and AMO physics, with a
        current focus on gas–surface scattering dynamics. Earlier projects spanned
        ultrafast electron dynamics, mesoscopic transport, and plasmonics. Projects below
        are listed in reverse chronological order.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        <p>“With more knowledge comes a deeper, more wonderful mystery, luring one on to penetrate deeper still.”</p>
        <footer>— Richard Feynman</footer>
      </blockquote>
    </header>

    <!-- ===== Publications ===== -->
    <section>
      <h2>Publications &amp; preprints</h2>
      <ol class="pub-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Mocherla, S. V.</strong>, &amp; Ramakrishnan, R.
          <em>Variational augmentation of Gaussian continuum basis sets for calculating atomic higher harmonic generation spectra.</em>
          arXiv preprint
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00732" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv:2307.00732</a>
          (2023).
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== Current ===== -->
    <section>
      <h2>Current research</h2>

      <article class="project">
        <header class="project-head">
          <h3>Inelastic scattering of noble gases from Au(111)</h3>
          <p class="meta mono">
            Ph.D. thesis · 02/2024 – present<br />
            Advisor: Prof. Pranav R. Shirhatti, Surface Dynamics Group, TIFR Hyderabad
          </p>
        </header>
        <p>
          Understanding how energy is transferred between gas-phase atoms and solid surfaces
          is a fundamental question in surface dynamics. My thesis focuses on the inelastic
          scattering of Kr, Ar, and Xe from Au(111) using molecular beam techniques.
          By measuring angular and energy distributions of scattered atoms we extract
          detailed information about energy-transfer mechanisms — including phonon
          excitation and trapping/desorption channels. The project also includes the
          development of fast high-voltage switches for charge-particle extraction in
          coincidence experiments.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="project">
        <header class="project-head">
          <h3>Machine-learning interatomic potentials for C-atom scattering on Au(111)</h3>
          <p class="meta mono">
            Visiting student · 01/2025 – 03/2025<br />
            Advisor: Prof. Alexander Kandratsenka, Max Planck Institute of Multidisciplinary Sciences, Göttingen
          </p>
        </header>
        <p>
          During a three-month visit to the Kandratsenka group, I performed plane-wave DFT
          calculations to generate training data for ML-based interatomic potentials (MLIPs).
          These potentials simulate carbon-atom scattering on Au(111) — a system where
          non-adiabatic dissipation via electronic friction plays a significant role. I also
          contributed to electronic-friction-based models for gas–surface scattering more broadly.
        </p>
        <p class="links">[ <a href="/docs/c_atom_scattering.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Report</a> ]</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <!-- ===== Earlier ===== -->
    <section>
      <h2>Earlier projects</h2>

      <article class="project">
        <header class="project-head">
          <h3>Optimized Gaussian basis sets for atomic high-harmonic generation</h3>
          <p class="meta mono">
            Junior Research Fellow · 09/2021 – 05/2023<br />
            Advisor: Prof. Raghunathan Ramakrishnan,
            <a href="https://moldis-group.github.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MolDis Group</a>, TIFR Hyderabad
          </p>
        </header>
        <p>
          High-harmonic generation (HHG) spectra probe ultrafast electron dynamics, but standard
          Gaussian basis sets struggle to describe the oscillatory continuum-like states involved
          in strong-field processes. I developed a variational method to augment Gaussian bases
          with continuum functions optimized for atomic HHG spectra computed via time-dependent
          configuration interaction (TDCI), making accurate HHG calculations tractable within a
          localized-basis framework.
        </p>
        <p class="links">[ <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00732" target="_blank" rel="noopener">arXiv:2307.00732</a> ]</p>
      </article>

      <article class="project two-col">
        <div class="proj-text">
          <header class="project-head">
            <h3>Pure dephasing and localization in open quantum systems</h3>
            <p class="meta mono">
              Final project, CHM-452 Quantum Dynamics<br />
              Advisor: Prof. Ignacio Franco, University of Rochester
            </p>
          </header>
          <p>
            I derived a Lindbladian master equation for a 1D tight-binding model interacting
            with a pure dephasing bath, then numerically solved the Liouville–von Neumann
            equation for disorder-averaged site populations in the long-time limit. The
            calculations showed that localization can be tuned via system–bath coupling.
          </p>
          <p class="links">[ <a href="https://github.com/vijaymocherla/Localisation-in-Open-quantum-systems.git" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code</a> ]</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/img/disordered_systems.webp" alt="Illustration of a disordered system" />
          <figcaption>Illustration of a disordered system.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project two-col">
        <div class="proj-text">
          <header class="project-head">
            <h3>Discrete-space random walks</h3>
            <p class="meta mono">
              Summer research internship<br />
              Advisor: Prof. Upendra Harbola, Indian Institute of Science, Bangalore
            </p>
          </header>
          <p>
            I worked through Nitzan and Spitzer to understand classical random walks on
            lattices and explored their connection to exciton transport models in mesoscopic
            systems through rudimentary numerical experiments.
          </p>
          <p class="links">[ <a href="https://github.com/vijaymocherla/Random-walk-on-2D-lattice.git" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Code</a> ]</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/img/2dwalk_simulation.webp" alt="A random walk on a 2D lattice" />
          <figcaption>A random walk on a 2D lattice.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project two-col">
        <div class="proj-text">
          <header class="project-head">
            <h3>Torsional disorder and exciton transport in conjugated polymers</h3>
            <p class="meta mono">
              Master's thesis<br />
              Sri Sathya Sai Institute of Higher Learning
            </p>
          </header>
          <p>
            I developed a model of an exciton coupled to rotational degrees of freedom of
            chromophore sub-units in a conjugated polymer. After deriving an analytic
            expression for the time-averaged transition probability in ordered chains, I
            used exact diagonalization to compute disorder-averaged quantities for static
            and dynamic torsional disorder, finding that exciton migration rates decreased
            inversely with polymer length.
          </p>
          <p class="links">[
            <a href="/docs/masters_thesis_FirstDraft.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Thesis</a> |
            <a href="/docs/masters_presentation.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Slides</a>
          ]</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/img/conjugated_polymer.webp" alt="Poly(p-phenylene)" />
          <figcaption>Poly(p-phenylene), a conjugated polymer.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>

      <article class="project two-col">
        <div class="proj-text">
          <header class="project-head">
            <h3>Surface plasmon polaritons in plasmonic quasi-crystals</h3>
            <p class="meta mono">
              Summer internship project<br />
              Advisor: Prof. Venu Gopal Achanta, TIFR Mumbai
            </p>
          </header>
          <p>
            I used electron-beam lithography and reactive-ion etching to fabricate
            quasi-periodic hole arrays on gold thin films, and used pump–probe spectroscopy
            to study the dynamics of surface-plasmon polaritons in these nanophotonic
            structures — relevant to broadband transmission and solar-energy applications.
          </p>
          <p class="links">[ <a href="/docs/plasmonic_quasicrystals_report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Report</a> ]</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <img src="/img/plqc.webp" alt="SEM image of a plasmonic quasi-crystal" />
          <figcaption>SEM image of a plasmonic quasi-crystal.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </article>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .research { padding-top: 3rem; }
  .page-head { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .page-head h1 { margin: 0.2rem 0 0.6rem; }
  .kicker {
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    margin: 0;
  }
  .lead { font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--text-muted); max-width: 64ch; }

  .pub-list { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
  .pub-list li { margin: 0.5rem 0; line-height: 1.55; }

  .project {
    padding: 1.4rem 0 0.4rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    margin-top: 1.4rem;
  }
  .project-head h3 { margin: 0 0 0.3rem; font-size: 1.18rem; }
  .meta {
    color: var(--text-faint);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }
  .meta a { color: var(--text-muted); }
  .links { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--text-muted); }

  .two-col {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 220px;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .two-col figure { margin: 0; }
  .two-col img {
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .two-col figcaption {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: 0.82rem;
    text-align: center;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .two-col { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .two-col figure { order: -1; max-width: 260px; margin: 0 auto; }
  }
</style>
